Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  cost of health care is a huge concern  for a lot of families and a florida  woman ended up with a hospital bill of  more than half a million dollars after  giving birth  despite having health insurance bc  bennett's story is part of our  continuing bill of the month partnership  with kaiser health news and npr  what happened was she switched health  insurance plans while her newborn was in  the nicu and she says that led to major  confusion over the billing  dorian is a healthy one-year-old now but  when he was first born prematurely in  november 2020 and rushed to the nicu  first time mom bc bennett was terrified  about his health i didn't even know if  he was born alive if he was still born  so i'm crying i'm very upset baby dorian  was in the hospital for about two months  before he was healthy enough to go home  then bennett got the hospital bill for  more than a half million dollars  i was  very upset when i saw the half a million  dollar bill  because i felt like i'd done everything  in my power to avoid them sending me  that huge bill  she had picked the hospital that was in  network for her united health care  insurance but in january 2021 while her  son was still in the nicu her employer  changed health plans to umr  instead of billing united health care  for 2020 and umr for 2021 the hospital  billed both policies for both years when  neither would cover the bill because of  the administrative error bennett was  charged more than 550 thousand dollars i  called the hospital several times just  to let them know hey you guys are  lumping the bill together you need to  split it out despite her calls the same  bill was sent again with a payment plan  of nearly 46 thousand dollars a month  which is ridiculous i don't have forty  six thousand dollars  to pay a month i was scared that i was  going to go into collections i hope that  by doing the story that they would  really think about  just healthcare from a holistic  standpoint  health is not just when you're in the  hospital and also has to do with how you  treat someone  after they've been discharged from the  hospital  the advent orlando hospital revised the  bill after kaiser health news contacted  it back in october bennett's more than  half a million dollar bill was then  updated to 300 total  in a statement united healthcare said it  did not receive the 2020 portion of the  hospital bill until this fall and it has  since paid that bill the hospital said  quote we apologize for the frustration  this caused  for future patients like mrs bennett who  may experience a change in insurance  during their treatment this case allowed  us to identify opportunities within our  system to improve the billing and  communications process close quote okay  that's good an opportunity the editor in  chief of kaiser health news dr elizabeth  rosenthal joins us now uh doctor good  morning to you  this is shocking for a lot of people i  can't imagine opening a bill and seeing  500 000  on it uh what should people do when  they're in a situation like this  well the problem here is that when  there's a snafu or a disagreement  between providers you know the patients  are left holding the bag and  you know this was not resolved until  nearly a year later after we called and  the weird thing is the aca the  affordable care act said the patients  had to be offered financial assistance  and many hospitals have these payment  plans but they're often automated so you  know if you take a bill of 550 000  and divide it by 12 and get 46 000 a  month that's absurd that's absurd they  should be embarrassed by that  it it is it is a big number pretty  eye-catching i don't know 46 000 a month  so you say it's the responsibility of  patience to uh to track down these  double billings or miss billings and  then sort it out so is the advice just  to make a lot of phone calls write a lot  of emails  well bc did everything in her power as  she said and i think what people need to  understand is the end of the year the  change over the year is is a perilous  time for patients healthcare has kind of  a y2k problem  you know your deductible resets your  plan may change the provider network may  change so you have to have your your  antenna up for all of those things  possibly and and the problem is computer  systems are not very good at kind of  saying oh yeah we understand what  happened here  bc was so what i suggest is first of all  don't pay the 46 000 a month even if you  have it which most people don't um the  second thing is go to your hr department  because your company has much more  leverage with these insurers than you do  individually and and last of all try and  send that payment plan to the hospital  ceo and say  really you expect people to pay this  um  come to bill of the month or contact  anna warner those are my two options uh  for sure yeah you can't deal with a  computer the computer doesn't care you  got to deal with a person if you can dr  rosenthal thank you very much

